<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Pilar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1820">1820</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentine-civil-wars">Argentine Civil Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Pilar was signed on 23 February 1820 by the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, Manuel de Sarratea, and the federalist caudillos <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-ramirez">Francisco Ramírez</a> of Entre Ríos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estanislao-lopez">Estanislao López</a> of Santa Fe, after their victory at Cepeda. It ended the central Directorate, accepted a federal organisation for the provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> and declared free navigation of the rivers. By ignoring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-gervasio-artigas">José Artigas</a>, it broke up the Federal League.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-pilar.jpg" alt="Treaty of Pilar" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gobernadores de Buenos Aires y de la Liga Federal · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TratadoPilar1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The treaty was a pact among the rulers of the provinces of Santa Fe, Entre Ríos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, signed in the town of Pilar, in Buenos Aires Province, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cepeda-1820">Battle of Cepeda</a> had brought down the national government. Its signatories were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estanislao-lopez">Estanislao López</a>, governor of Santa Fe, the caudillo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-ramirez">Francisco Ramírez</a> for Entre Ríos and the provisional governor Manuel de Sarratea for Buenos Aires. José Gervasio Artigas, leader of the Free Peoples' League, who had fought alongside Santa Fe and Entre Ríos against the centralist government in Buenos Aires, had shortly before been defeated by the Brazilian-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tacuarembo">Battle of Tacuarembó</a>, on the Eastern Bank.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty proclaimed national unity and an end to hostilities between the provinces. Military forces were to withdraw from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, a general amnesty was granted to those persecuted for politics, and the signatories were to have free navigation of the Paraná and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> rivers. Each was to send deputies to a congress at San Lorenzo, in Santa Fe, sixty days later to decide on a federal form of government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Artigas, left out of the agreement, denounced it as treason by his allies; López answered that it served the common good. The Treaty of Benegas soon followed, binding Santa Fe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> to defend each other against an expanding Entre Ríos, and two years later came the Quadrilateral Treaty, which joined Corrientes Province to the three signatories of Pilar. The treaty is regarded as the foundation of the federal organisation of the country. The preamble of the Argentine Constitution of 1853 refers to it as one of the pre-existing pacts that the constitution fulfilled.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Pilar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Artigas's defeat at Tacuarembó in 1820, his allies Francisco Ramírez and Estanislao López made peace with Buenos Aires in the <b>Treaty of Pilar</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/federal-league">Federal League</a></li><li><blockquote>After defeating Buenos Aires at Cepeda in 1820 and signing the <b>Treaty of Pilar</b>, Francisco Ramírez broke with his former leader José Artigas of the Federal League and defeated him.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-entre-rios">Republic of Entre Ríos</a></li></ul></section>
